-When a custom command is provided, BentoML will launch a single process for that Service using your command. It will set the ``PORT`` environment variable (and ``BENTOML_HOST``/``BENTOML_PORT`` for the entry Service). Your process must listen on the provided ``PORT`` and serve HTTP endpoints. Server-level options like CORS/SSL/timeouts defined in BentoML won't apply automatically—configure them in your own server if needed.
+When a custom command is provided, BentoML launches a single process for that Service using your command. It sets the ``PORT`` environment variable (and ``BENTOML_HOST``/``BENTOML_PORT`` for the entry Service). Your process must listen on the provided ``PORT`` and serve HTTP endpoints.
+
+BentoML then runs an in-process reverse proxy in front of that process: incoming requests to the Service are forwarded to the HTTP server started by your command. Before accepting traffic, BentoML polls the health endpoint of your server, so make sure it responds to health checks once it is ready. The port your subprocess is expected to bind is controlled by the ``proxy_port`` configuration field (default ``8000``):
+
+.. code-block:: yaml
+
+    services:
+      http:
+        proxy_port: 8000
+
+Server-level options like CORS/SSL/timeouts defined in BentoML won't apply automatically—configure them in your own server if needed.
 
